### 2. Fix handleAsyncFailure Status Silencing (NC-6)
Your current failure handler accidentally deletes the master tracking record right before trying to update the error message on it, causing the batch to disappear without a trace.
**File:** JournalRequestServiceImpl.java
Locate your handleAsyncFailure method and **replace it with this updated version**:
```java
    private void handleAsyncFailure(String batchId, String creatorId, String requestId, String message) {
        try {
            // 1. First: Delete orphaned journal request rows only so GL stays balanced
            jdbcTemplate.update("DELETE FROM JOURNAL_REQUEST WHERE BATCH_ID = ?", batchId);

            // 2. Then: UPSERT the master record with error status (don't delete it!)
            int updated = jdbcTemplate.update(
                "UPDATE JOURNAL_BATCH_MASTER SET BATCH_STATUS = 'ERROR', EXECUTOR_REMARKS = ? WHERE BATCH_ID = ?",
                message, batchId);

            // If master record doesn't exist yet, insert it so there's a permanent audit trace
            if (updated == 0) {
                jdbcTemplate.update(
                    "INSERT INTO JOURNAL_BATCH_MASTER (BATCH_ID, BATCH_STATUS, EXECUTOR_REMARKS, REQ_DATE) VALUES (?, 'ERROR', ?, SYSTIMESTAMP)",
                    batchId, message);
            }
        } catch (DataAccessException e) {
            log.error("CLEANUP_CRITICAL_FAIL | Batch: {} | Cannot record failure state", batchId);
        }
        progressService.sendProgress(batchId, creatorId, 0, "FAILED", message);
    }

